/*===============================================================================
📘 SYSTEMVERILOG ASSERTIONS — CONCURRENT ASSERTIONS
===============================================================================

🔹 1. WHAT ARE CONCURRENT ASSERTIONS?
Concurrent assertions are statements that check **design behavior over time** (i.e., across multiple clock cycles). They run **in parallel** with the design, 
hence the name "concurrent".

These are used to ensure that **time-dependent rules** (like handshakes, timing relationships, or sequences of events) occur correctly. 
For example: “If `req` goes high, `ack` must go high within 3 clock cycles.”

✔ Used for:  
  - Protocol checks (REQ/ACK, VALID/READY)
  - Timing checks (delays, sequencing)
  - Multi-cycle verification rules
  - Temporal relationships (A before B, etc.)

================================================================================
🔹 2. WHY DV ENGINEERS NEED CONCURRENT ASSERTIONS
================================================================================
A Design Verification (DV) Engineer not only checks if outputs are *functionally* correct — they must also ensure **timing correctness** and **protocol integrity**.

Concurrent assertions:
  ✅ Run automatically on every clock edge  
  ✅ Detect handshake / timing violations  
  ✅ Work in both **simulation** and **formal** tools  
  ✅ Greatly reduce manual `if–else` debugging  
  ✅ Are essential in every **UVM testbench** via interfaces or monitors  

================================================================================
🔹 3. BASIC SYNTAX
================================================================================
📘 Syntax:
assert property (@(posedge clk) <sequence_or_rule>)
  else <fail_statement>;
✔ Each concurrent assertion is clock-based.
✔ The expression is sampled on the posedge clk (or other edge).
✔ It monitors the rule every cycle until the simulation ends.

================================================================================
🔹 4. PROPERTY DECLARATION
To express a time-based rule, we use a property block.

📘 Syntax:

property <property_name>;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!reset_n)
  <sequence>;
endproperty

assert property (<property_name>)
  else $error("Rule violated!");

🧩 Explanation:
property ... endproperty → defines a reusable rule
@(posedge clk) → evaluates only on the rising edge
disable iff (!reset_n) → temporarily disables checking while reset is active
assert property() → enforces the rule and reports failures


💬 Common Beginner Doubt:
What does disable iff (!reset_n) mean?
👉 It means: “Disable this assertion if condition is true.”
So when reset_n = 0, the rule is not checked (to avoid false failures during reset).
When reset is de-asserted (reset_n = 1), the assertion becomes active again.

================================================================================
🔹 5. KEY OPERATORS (used 95% of the time)
Operator	                  Say it like...	              Meaning
`	                               ->`	               “if… then (overlapped)”
`	                               =>`	               “if… then next (non-overlapped)”
##N	                        “after N cycles”	        RHS must hold N cycles later
##[m:n] 	                “within m to n cycles”	    RHS must occur within this range
disable iff (cond)	          “turn off if”	          Temporarily disables assertion


💬 Common Beginner Doubt:

Are |-> and |=> the same?
❌ No. They differ by 1 cycle:
|-> → RHS may start in the same cycle (overlapped).
|=> → RHS starts in the next cycle (non-overlapped).


📊 Visualization:
| Cycle | req | ack | |-> | |=> |
|-------|-----|-----|-------|-------|
| t0 | 1 | 1 | ✅ Pass | ❌ Fail |
| t1 | 1 | 0 → 1 next | ✅ Pass | ✅ Pass |
| t2 | 1 | 0 (never) | ❌ Fail | ❌ Fail |

================================================================================
🔹 6. SIMPLE EXAMPLES
✔ Inline property
assert property (@(posedge clk) (req1 || req2));
Checks that at least one of req1 or req2 is 1 at every posedge clk.


✔ Named property
property p_req_ack;
  @(posedge clk) disable iff (!reset_n)
  req |-> ##[1:3] ack;
endproperty

assert property (p_req_ack)
  else $error("ACK not seen within 3 cycles of REQ");
If req goes high, ack must follow within 1–3 cycles.

Disabled when reset_n = 0.

================================================================================
🔹 7. WHERE CONCURRENT ASSERTIONS ARE WRITTEN
Inside modules, interfaces, or packages
Often placed inside clocking blocks (in UVM interfaces)
Can be reused in multiple testbenches
Checked during simulation and formal verification

================================================================================
🔹 8. PRACTICAL DV APPLICATIONS
DV Engineers use concurrent assertions for:

Handshake protocols: REQ/ACK, VALID/READY timing
Reset sequencing: outputs must remain 0 until reset released
Bus verification: AXI, AHB, APB timing windows
Coverage checks: to confirm protocol scenarios were exercised

================================================================================

🔹9. SUMMARY STATEMENT
Concurrent assertions are the backbone of Assertion-Based Verification (ABV) in real-world environments.
They verify timing relationships, handshakes, and sequencing over time, and help Design Verification Engineers catch protocol bugs early with precision.

===============================================================================*/
